#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166452 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "c5e60742-2571-5100-96be-a7578e6dda07" :question/math-academy-id "e-21540" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the general solution of the system." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $a,b$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative of $\\mathbf{x}_p$ with respect to $t$ gives\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute $\\mathbf{x}_p$ and $\\mathbf{x}'_p$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n7\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3a + b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3a + b - 1 \\\\ b - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{3a + b - 1 = 7a; b - 3 = 7b\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nb - 7b = 3 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad b = -\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $a$, we have\n\n$$\n3a - \\dfrac12 - 1 = 7a \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad a = -\\dfrac38\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAnd thus, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "6f03c3e2-26dc-5ef3-90d9-9b80feac1ad4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21541" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $c_1,c_2$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nNext, we find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$. Substituting $t = 0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\cdot e^{0} \\\\ e^{0}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0^{2} - 0 + 4 \\\\ -0^{2} + 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + 4 \\\\ c_{2} + 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{c_{1} + 4 = 6; c_{2} + 2 = 1\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $c_1 = 2$. From the second equation, we have $c_2 = -1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eecbfc4-f7eb-5d4c-bea6-8fd21839804c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of a Particular Solution for Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "a5c44ae4-e55b-512f-ac1c-46f62950c1f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21543" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t) - \\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$, where $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ a_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51834713-edc2-5af3-a0dc-fb54ea4040c6" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "aabc0f35-fdfc-573a-8d86-ada823a380a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21544" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-2x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t) + 4t^{2} + t - 2; x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - t^{2} + 5t + 6$\nWrite the system of linear ODEs in matrix form." :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = (-2) \\cdot x_{1}(t) + (-1) \\cdot x_{2}(t) + 4 \\cdot t^{2} + 1 \\cdot t + (-2); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3 \\cdot x_{1}(t) + 0 \\cdot x_{2}(t) + (-1) \\cdot t^{2} + 5 \\cdot t + 6\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51834713-edc2-5af3-a0dc-fb54ea4040c6" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a603db14-0f66-5114-b73d-4a81108e559c" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164033} {:answer-field/id #uuid "c04fef2b-e214-5405-b695-49022bf6c23c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164034} {:answer-field/id #uuid "d0d55fdb-0228-5247-8794-4dd7226c75a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164035} {:answer-field/id #uuid "daa851e2-39ab-5170-80c7-31369e65152d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164036} {:answer-field/id #uuid "e96be42b-7f6b-54e8-a653-f6cbeb4ff961" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164037} {:answer-field/id #uuid "52218fc7-3c42-530b-8676-0fe430501706" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164038} {:answer-field/id #uuid "54056f04-4689-59df-ba3e-649b6429f44a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164039} {:answer-field/id #uuid "70700e29-c77d-5aa8-a9d2-2a0e3cb8a9c7" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164040}] :question/id #uuid "90dda3ee-b3fe-59fe-9f9b-bbb2e1549a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-329268" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t) + 3t + 2; x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t) - t$\nThe systems of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| $x^{′}(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $x(t) +$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  | $t +$ |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"complementary-vs-particular-solutions\"></a>"}]}
